WEBVTT
NOTE how-deep-can-a-sperm-whale-go - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.759
A sperm whale can dive to a depth of 2,992
meters, a figure recorded by digital

00:00:05.759 --> 00:00:09.915
acoustic tags and published by the Census
of Marine Life.

00:00:09.915 --> 00:00:15.186
This depth represents a pressure of nearly
300 atmospheres, yet these air-breathing

00:00:15.186 --> 00:00:18.805
mammals regularly descend into the
midnight zone to hunt.

00:00:18.805 --> 00:00:23.612
While 2,992 meters is the confirmed SPERM
WHALE RECORD, it does not settle the

00:00:23.612 --> 00:00:28.296
question of their absolute biological
limit or how they survive the crushing

00:00:28.296 --> 00:00:29.467
weight of the deep.

00:00:29.467 --> 00:00:31.744
how deep can a sperm whale go?

00:00:31.744 --> 00:00:37.758
To understand how we know a sperm whale
can reach 2,992 meters, we must look at

00:00:37.758 --> 00:00:40.347
the technology used to track them.

00:00:40.347 --> 00:00:45.382
For most of the twentieth century, our
understanding of whale depth was based on

00:00:45.382 --> 00:00:48.907
indirect evidence from whaling or
accidental encounters.

00:00:48.907 --> 00:00:54.621
This changed with the development of the
DTAG, or digital acoustic recording tag,

00:00:54.621 --> 00:00:59.419
by researchers at the Woods Hole
Oceanographic Institution, or WHOI.

00:00:59.419 --> 00:01:05.161
A DTAG is a compact device equipped with a
suite of sensors, including pressure

00:01:05.161 --> 00:01:08.213
gauges, accelerometers, and magnetometers.

00:01:08.213 --> 00:01:12.880
The TAGGING process begins with a careful
APPROACH by a research vessel.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:17.853
Using a 12-meter carbon fiber POLE
DEPLOYMENT, the team achieves a SUCTION

00:01:17.853 --> 00:01:20.811
ATTACHMENT of the tag onto the whale’s
back.

00:01:20.811 --> 00:01:26.730
Once secured, the device begins continuous
DATA LOGGING of the animal's movements and

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:31.813
the surrounding environment, sampling
audio at rates up to 192 kilohertz.

00:01:31.813 --> 00:01:36.875
The PRESSURE SENSOR inside the tag is the
critical instrument for depth measurement.

00:01:36.875 --> 00:01:41.595
As the whale descends, the increasing
weight of the water column exerts force on

00:01:41.595 --> 00:01:43.837
a silicon diaphragm within the sensor.

00:01:43.837 --> 00:01:49.329
This force causes a measurable VOLTAGE
CHANGE through electrical resistance,

00:01:49.329 --> 00:01:52.941
which the tag’s computer converts into
DEPTH DATA.

00:01:52.941 --> 00:01:57.287
Because the tag records data multiple
times per second, researchers can

00:01:57.287 --> 00:02:00.776
reconstruct a high-resolution profile of
the entire dive.

00:02:00.776 --> 00:02:04.258
This is how the record of 2,992 meters was
captured.

00:02:04.258 --> 00:02:09.615
The tag eventually releases its suction
grip, floats to the surface, and emits a

00:02:09.615 --> 00:02:13.432
radio signal so researchers can retrieve
the stored data.

00:02:13.432 --> 00:02:18.557
This method has transformed marine biology
from a science of surface observations

00:02:18.557 --> 00:02:23.430
into a science of deep-sea tracking,
allowing us to map the three-dimensional

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:26.024
path of a whale as it navigates the dark.

00:02:26.024 --> 00:02:33.034
While 2,992 METERS is the RECORDED
MAXIMUM, we maintain a 95% confidence that

00:02:33.034 --> 00:02:37.859
most extreme dives fall within the
2,250/3,000 range.

00:02:37.859 --> 00:02:42.395
It is important to note that we have only
tagged a small fraction of the global

00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:43.027
population.

00:02:43.027 --> 00:02:49.430
NOAA Ocean Exploration notes that regular
foraging dives are more frequently

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:52.632
recorded between 600 and 2,250 meters.

00:02:52.632 --> 00:02:57.342
The variation in these figures often
depends on the local bathymetry and the

00:02:57.342 --> 00:02:59.635
depth at which their prey is located.

00:02:59.635 --> 00:03:04.817
In some regions, the seafloor may not even
reach 3,000 meters, effectively capping

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:09.304
the whale's potential dive depth
regardless of its physical capability.

00:03:09.304 --> 00:03:15.150
We must view the 2,992 meter record as a
verified minimum for the species'

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:18.547
potential, rather than an absolute
ceiling.

00:03:18.547 --> 00:03:23.372
The data suggests that these animals are
not just visiting these depths, but are

00:03:23.372 --> 00:03:28.500
operating within a well-defined ecological
niche that spans several kilometers of the

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:29.827
vertical water column.

00:03:29.827 --> 00:03:36.662
At a depth of 2,992 meters, the pressure
is approximately 300 times greater than at

00:03:36.662 --> 00:03:37.651
the surface.

00:03:37.651 --> 00:03:42.303
For a human, or any unpressurized vessel,
this would be catastrophic.

00:03:42.303 --> 00:03:47.629
To survive this, the sperm whale has
evolved a suite of biological adaptations.

00:03:47.629 --> 00:03:52.955
According to the Smithsonian Ocean portal,
one of the most critical is the ability to

00:03:52.955 --> 00:03:54.333
collapse the rib cage.

00:03:54.333 --> 00:03:59.214
Unlike humans, whose rigid chests would
crack under the weight of the deep ocean,

00:03:59.214 --> 00:04:02.648
a sperm whale’s ribs are connected by
flexible cartilage.

00:04:02.648 --> 00:04:07.600
This allows the lungs to fold down
completely, which prevents the air inside

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:12.813
from being forced into the bloodstream
under high pressure, a process that helps

00:04:12.813 --> 00:04:17.504
avoid the nitrogen narcosis and
decompression sickness that plague human

00:04:17.504 --> 00:04:17.960
divers.

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:22.523
By eliminating the air space, the whale
becomes essentially incompressible, its

00:04:22.523 --> 00:04:26.104
internal tissues matching the pressure of
the surrounding sea.

00:04:26.104 --> 00:04:29.951
Another vital adaptation is the whale's
internal oxygen storage.

00:04:29.951 --> 00:04:34.940
While humans store most of their oxygen in
their lungs, deep-diving whales store it

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:36.563
in their blood and muscles.

00:04:36.563 --> 00:04:41.810
The SPERM WHALE has a high concentration
of hemoglobin in its blood and an even

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:45.464
higher concentration of myoglobin in its
muscle tissue.

00:04:45.464 --> 00:04:51.047
Myoglobin is a protein that binds oxygen,
and in sperm whales, it is found at levels

00:04:51.047 --> 00:04:53.971
10 times higher than in terrestrial
mammals.

00:04:53.971 --> 00:04:58.336
This allows them to remain submerged for
90 minutes or more, even though their

00:04:58.336 --> 00:05:02.309
COLLAPSIBLE LUNGS are non-functional for
the duration of the deep dive.

00:05:02.309 --> 00:05:06.776
The SPERMACETI ORGAN, located in the
massive head, also plays a role in this

00:05:06.776 --> 00:05:11.595
specialized anatomy, though its primary
function is related to sound and buoyancy.

00:05:11.595 --> 00:05:16.605
During the dive, the whale can shunt blood
away from its extremities, prioritizing

00:05:16.605 --> 00:05:21.616
the oxygen supply for the brain and heart,
ensuring that critical functions remain

00:05:21.616 --> 00:05:25.160
active even as the body endures extreme
cold and pressure.

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:29.818
The efficiency of this system is what
allows the whale to perform what

00:05:29.818 --> 00:05:32.013
researchers call an AEROBIC DIVE.

00:05:32.013 --> 00:05:36.643
By managing their oxygen stores carefully,
they can stay below the surface for long

00:05:36.643 --> 00:05:39.432
periods without building up excessive
lactic acid.

00:05:39.432 --> 00:05:41.252
This is a delicate balance.

00:05:41.252 --> 00:05:46.173
If a whale stays down too long and
exhausts its oxygen, it must switch to

00:05:46.173 --> 00:05:51.565
ANAEROBIC metabolism, which requires a
much longer recovery time at the surface.

00:05:51.565 --> 00:05:56.537
Data from WHOI suggests that most sperm
whale dives stay within this AEROBIC

00:05:56.537 --> 00:06:01.050
limit, ensuring they can dive again
shortly after a brief interval of

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:02.685
breathing at the surface.

00:06:02.685 --> 00:06:07.333
This capacity for rapid "re-diving" is
essential for a predator that must spend

00:06:07.333 --> 00:06:09.392
hours each day hunting in the deep.

00:06:09.392 --> 00:06:14.140
The whale is not just surviving the dive;
it is maintaining a high level of athletic

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:18.549
performance in an environment that would
be lethal to almost any other mammal.

00:06:18.549 --> 00:06:23.849
The sperm whale is named for the
spermaceti organ, a massive cavity in its

00:06:23.849 --> 00:06:29.435
head filled with up to 1,900 liters of a
waxy substance called spermaceti oil.

00:06:29.435 --> 00:06:33.682
For decades, scientists have debated the
function of this organ.

00:06:33.682 --> 00:06:39.124
One leading hypothesis, supported by the
Monterey Bay Aquarium Research Institute,

00:06:39.124 --> 00:06:42.443
or MBARI, is that it acts as a buoyancy
regulator.

00:06:42.443 --> 00:06:47.314
As the whale begins its descent into the
colder waters of the BATHYPELAGIC zone,

00:06:47.314 --> 00:06:52.125
also known as THE MIDNIGHT ZONE, which
NOAA defines as the region between 1,000

00:06:52.125 --> 00:06:56.814
to 4,000 meters, it may circulate cold
seawater through its nasal passages to

00:06:56.814 --> 00:06:57.605
cool the oil.

00:06:57.605 --> 00:07:02.704
This cooling process is facilitated by a
complex heat exchange system involving the

00:07:02.704 --> 00:07:07.742
right and left nasal passages, which can
selectively expose the oil to the ambient

00:07:07.742 --> 00:07:09.216
temperature of the deep.

00:07:09.216 --> 00:07:13.212
As the spermaceti oil cools, it solidifies
and becomes denser.

00:07:13.212 --> 00:07:18.498
This increase in density would provide the
whale with BUOYANCY CONTROL, helping it

00:07:18.498 --> 00:07:22.043
sink without having to expend energy
swimming downward.

00:07:22.043 --> 00:07:26.879
When the whale is ready to return to the
surface, increased blood flow to the organ

00:07:26.879 --> 00:07:30.667
could warm the wax, melting it and making
the whale more buoyant.

00:07:30.667 --> 00:07:35.135
This theory is still a subject of active
research, as it is difficult to measure

00:07:35.135 --> 00:07:38.096
the temperature of the oil in a living,
diving whale.

00:07:38.096 --> 00:07:42.891
However, the sheer size of the
organ—occupying nearly one-third of the

00:07:42.891 --> 00:07:48.507
whale's body length—suggests it plays a
fundamental role in its deep-sea lifestyle

00:07:48.507 --> 00:07:52.480
alongside its COLLAPSIBLE LUNGS and high
MYOGLOBIN levels.

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:58.183
These DIVE ADAPTATIONS are what make such
extreme depths accessible, turning the

00:07:58.183 --> 00:08:02.960
whale into a biological submarine capable
of passive depth changes.

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:07.778
If this buoyancy theory is correct, it
explains how a creature the size of a

00:08:07.778 --> 00:08:12.787
school bus can descend to nearly 3,000
meters and return without exhausting its

00:08:12.787 --> 00:08:13.675
oxygen supply.

00:08:13.675 --> 00:08:17.092
Every movement in the deep ocean is a
calculation of energy.

00:08:17.092 --> 00:08:21.535
By using a passive system to change its
density, the sperm whale preserves its

00:08:21.535 --> 00:08:23.813
muscle-bound oxygen for the actual hunt.

00:08:23.813 --> 00:08:28.624
This energy conservation is what makes the
sperm whale one of the most successful

00:08:28.624 --> 00:08:33.078
predators in the ocean's interior,
allowing it to spend the majority of its

00:08:33.078 --> 00:08:37.355
life in a realm where food is scarce and
the environment is unforgiving.

00:08:37.355 --> 00:08:43.005
We know sperm whales are hunting at these
depths because the DTAGs also record

00:08:43.005 --> 00:08:43.440
sound.

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:47.616
In the total darkness of the deep ocean,
eyes are of limited use.

00:08:47.616 --> 00:08:52.475
Instead, the sperm whale uses the most
powerful biological sonar on Earth.

00:08:52.475 --> 00:08:57.164
By forcing air through specialized
structures in its nasal passage known as

00:08:57.164 --> 00:08:59.915
phonic lips, the whale produces loud
clicks.

00:08:59.915 --> 00:09:05.856
The CLICK EMISSION travels through the
spermaceti organ, which focuses the sound

00:09:05.856 --> 00:09:06.747
into a beam.

00:09:06.747 --> 00:09:11.445
When the sound hits an object, like a
giant squid, it creates a TARGET

00:09:11.445 --> 00:09:14.197
REFLECTION that echoes back to the whale.

00:09:14.197 --> 00:09:19.478
This sophisticated PREY DETECTION system
allows the whale to "see" with sound

00:09:19.478 --> 00:09:24.347
through ECHOLOCATION, identifying targets
from hundreds of meters away.

00:09:24.347 --> 00:09:29.573
The interval between the click and the
echo tells the whale exactly how far away

00:09:29.573 --> 00:09:30.357
the prey is.

00:09:30.357 --> 00:09:35.226
As the whale closes in on its target, the
rate of clicking increases, creating a

00:09:35.226 --> 00:09:39.790
sound known as a "terminal buzz." By
syncing this audio data with the depth

00:09:39.790 --> 00:09:44.962
data from the pressure sensor, researchers
can confirm that sperm whales are actively

00:09:44.962 --> 00:09:47.275
foraging at the bottom of their dives.

00:09:47.275 --> 00:09:52.829
We have recorded these terminal buzzes at
depths exceeding 2,000 meters, proving

00:09:52.829 --> 00:09:58.453
that the descent to 2,992 meters is not a
random event, but a targeted search for

00:09:58.453 --> 00:09:58.800
food.

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:04.832
This depth far exceeds the 1,133 meter
CABLE record from the mid-twentieth

00:10:04.832 --> 00:10:11.109
century and the 2,250 meter COMMON
foraging depth compared to the 2,992 meter

00:10:11.109 --> 00:10:11.680
RECORD.

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:16.443
The intensity of these clicks can reach
230 decibels, making them the loudest

00:10:16.443 --> 00:10:18.299
sounds produced by any animal.

00:10:18.299 --> 00:10:23.168
The primary targets of these hunts are
cephalopods, including the giant squid.

00:10:23.168 --> 00:10:27.282
While we rarely see these battles
directly, the evidence is written on the

00:10:27.282 --> 00:10:28.283
whales themselves.

00:10:28.283 --> 00:10:32.949
Many adult sperm whales bear circular
scars from the suckers of large squid.

00:10:32.949 --> 00:10:38.147
Furthermore, the stomach contents of
stranded whales often contain thousands of

00:10:38.147 --> 00:10:39.792
indigestible squid beaks.

00:10:39.792 --> 00:10:43.723
By identifying the species of squid from
these beaks, marine biologists at

00:10:43.723 --> 00:10:47.654
institutions like the Smithsonian can
determine the depth ranges where the

00:10:47.654 --> 00:10:49.248
whales must have been feeding.

00:10:49.248 --> 00:10:54.877
The DATA SOURCES from WHOI and NOAA
consistently point to 2,992 and 2,250

00:10:54.877 --> 00:11:00.891
meters respectively for these deep-sea
predators, confirming that their entire

00:11:00.891 --> 00:11:03.667
life cycle is tethered to the abyss.

00:11:03.667 --> 00:11:08.365
What gets me is the amount of water above
you, and how completely unreachable the

00:11:08.365 --> 00:11:10.221
surface becomes from down there.

00:11:10.221 --> 00:11:15.426
Before the era of digital tags, our
evidence for sperm whale depth came from a

00:11:15.426 --> 00:11:19.229
much more destructive source: submarine
telegraph cables.

00:11:19.229 --> 00:11:24.129
In the mid-twentieth century, as global
communication networks expanded across the

00:11:24.129 --> 00:11:28.312
ocean floor, repair crews began finding
whales entangled in the lines.

00:11:28.312 --> 00:11:34.195
In 1955, a report by geologist Bruce
Heezen documented a sperm whale entangled

00:11:34.195 --> 00:11:39.400
in a CABLE at a depth of 1,133 meters off
the coast of South America.

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:43.619
At the time, this was one of the deepest
confirmed records for any mammal.

00:11:43.619 --> 00:11:49.763
Later, in 1991, SONAR observations began
to provide more dynamic data, leading

00:11:49.763 --> 00:11:56.065
eventually to the first DTAG deployments
in 2000 by researchers Mark Johnson and

00:11:56.065 --> 00:11:57.011
Peter Tyack.

00:11:57.011 --> 00:12:01.934
While these cable entanglements provided a
physical proof of depth, they were

00:12:01.934 --> 00:12:02.573
imprecise.

00:12:02.573 --> 00:12:06.767
It was often unclear if the whale had
become entangled at that depth or if it

00:12:06.767 --> 00:12:09.491
had been dragged there by the weight of
the cable.

00:12:09.491 --> 00:12:14.344
However, these incidents established the
sperm whale’s reputation as a deep diver

00:12:14.344 --> 00:12:16.920
long before we had the sensors to prove
it.

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:23.033
To put the modern 2,992 meter record in
perspective, it is more than three times

00:12:23.033 --> 00:12:28.917
the height of the BURJ KHALIFA, the
world's tallest building, which stands at

00:12:28.917 --> 00:12:29.757
828 meters.

00:12:29.757 --> 00:12:34.164
The whale is essentially traveling the
length of three and a half skyscrapers

00:12:34.164 --> 00:12:38.743
straight down into the earth and back up
on a single breath, enduring a pressure

00:12:38.743 --> 00:12:43.093
change that would crush any man-made
structure not specifically designed for

00:12:43.093 --> 00:12:43.608
the deep.

00:12:43.608 --> 00:12:48.780
The transition from cable entanglements to
acoustic tagging represents a shift in how

00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:49.875
we know the ocean.

00:12:49.875 --> 00:12:55.095
We moved from accidental, often fatal
encounters to precise, non-invasive

00:12:55.095 --> 00:12:55.953
observation.

00:12:55.953 --> 00:13:01.317
This has allowed us to see that the 1,133
meter cable record was actually a

00:13:01.317 --> 00:13:04.749
conservative estimate of the whale's true
range.

00:13:04.749 --> 00:13:09.268
Modern data shows they regularly double
that depth as part of their daily routine,

00:13:09.268 --> 00:13:12.189
a feat that was once thought impossible
for a mammal.

00:13:12.189 --> 00:13:17.283
This evolution of evidence highlights the
importance of technological advancement in

00:13:17.283 --> 00:13:21.347
our quest to understand the planet's least
accessible environments.

00:13:21.347 --> 00:13:26.707
When examining the data, it is important
to distinguish between "regular" behavior

00:13:26.707 --> 00:13:28.211
and "maximum" behavior.

00:13:28.211 --> 00:13:33.312
Most scientific literature, including
publications from NOAA, focuses on the

00:13:33.312 --> 00:13:38.750
2,250 meter range because that is where
the majority of the population spends its

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:39.085
time.

00:13:39.085 --> 00:13:44.857
The 2,992 meter figure, also cited by
GUINNESS World Records, is an outlier—a

00:13:44.857 --> 00:13:50.554
record-breaking performance likely
achieved by a large male in peak physical

00:13:50.554 --> 00:13:51.304
condition.

00:13:51.304 --> 00:13:56.688
In the edit, you will see that the depth
profile of a sperm whale dive is often

00:13:56.688 --> 00:14:02.276
shaped like a "U," with a rapid descent, a
long period of foraging at a consistent

00:14:02.276 --> 00:14:04.116
depth, and a steady ascent.

00:14:04.116 --> 00:14:08.819
This "U" shape is the hallmark of a
focused, energy-efficient hunter.

00:14:08.819 --> 00:14:13.603
The evidence we have for the sperm whale's
maximum depth is limited by the duration

00:14:13.603 --> 00:14:16.888
of our observations and the number of
tagged individuals.

00:14:16.888 --> 00:14:23.508
While 2,992 METERS is the highest figure
currently recorded by a DTAG, it does not

00:14:23.508 --> 00:14:29.160
necessarily represent the absolute
physiological limit of the species.

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:33.251
Furthermore, other species have since
surpassed this record.

00:14:33.251 --> 00:14:38.023
The Cuvier’s beaked whale has been
recorded at depths far beyond 3,000

00:14:38.023 --> 00:14:41.432
meters, with one individual reaching 3,700
meters.

00:14:41.432 --> 00:14:46.157
Our knowledge of "the deepest" is always a
moving target, defined by the latest

00:14:46.157 --> 00:14:47.592
successful tag recovery.

00:14:47.592 --> 00:14:51.898
We must acknowledge that there may be
sperm whales diving even deeper in parts

00:14:51.898 --> 00:14:56.094
of the ocean we have yet to monitor,
hidden by the sheer scale of the global

00:14:56.094 --> 00:14:56.315
sea.

00:14:56.315 --> 00:15:00.809
We know the sperm whale can reach 2,992
meters because we have measured the

00:15:00.809 --> 00:15:04.704
pressure on its back and heard the sound
of its hunt in the dark.

00:15:04.704 --> 00:15:09.477
This figure is not a guess, but a data
point captured by sensors that survive

00:15:09.477 --> 00:15:10.469
where we cannot.

00:15:10.469 --> 00:15:14.355
As tagging technology improves and we
monitor more of the ocean, our

00:15:14.355 --> 00:15:19.040
understanding of these deep-sea journeys
will continue to evolve, likely revealing

00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:23.040
that the limits of the deep are even
further than we currently record.

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:27.709
The sperm whale remains a sentinel of the
abyss, a bridge between the world of air

00:15:27.709 --> 00:15:29.701
and the world of crushing pressure.
